\section{Discussion and limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What the source models buy.}
The answer is conditional.
When targets resemble the sources, they buy about a fifth of the error of stratified sampling, which already uses the sources' item difficulties.
When every target is stronger than every source, predictors fitted on the sources are biased toward the source mean and what remains over stratified sampling is at most a tenth; a difference correction reduces that bias without removing it, and a weight chosen on an inner split repairs it only in part.
The answer depends on the distance above the sources and the number of source models, and the ordering gain is confined to interpolation and lineage.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Baselines.}
The anchor-point, tinyBenchmarks-style and metabench-style methods are our re-implementations; the anchor clustering is light ($k$-means with one start and $25$ iterations); a stronger clustering, run on the seven leaderboard benchmarks only, changes the ratios by at most $0.10$ and no conclusion; the stronger runs use different draws and have no intervals, so differences of a few percent are within draw noise.
We did not run the assessor~\citep{pacchiardi2024100}, active evaluation acquisition~\citep{li2025active}, SubLIME~\citep{saranathan-etal-2025-sublime} or the released code of the earlier methods, so nothing here is a statement about those.
\emph{Data.}
The $13$ benchmarks are not $13$ replications (Section~\ref{sec:setup}), and our intervals reflect the sampling of target families only, not of benchmarks or source models.
Families are derived from model names by keyword; cells with few targets are marked thin.
\emph{Selection and tuning.}
Rows named best were chosen on the targets and draws their intervals use; the leave-one-benchmark-out rows are the outcome-independent alternative; the larger-gap inner split was chosen after the main result and is a diagnosis, not a recipe.
\emph{Scope.}
Items are counted, not priced; correctness is binary; no live model was evaluated; $n=100$ is a different share of the items in each benchmark.
