Regressing the extrapolation bias of the model-based estimators ($n=100$, pooled over benchmarks) on the target's distance above the lowest target gives a slope per standard deviation of $-0.27$ for ridge on a balanced design, against $-0.61$ for the IRT model and $-0.88$ for kNN; on a uniform design ridge's slope is $-0.54$, so the advantage belongs to ridge with a balanced design, which is less biased there, not unbiased. We did not test why.

The weight $\lambda$ of the correction is chosen on an inner split of the sources that mimics the outer split.
In interpolation this helps ($0.65$ $[0.63,0.67]$ tuned against $0.74$ $[0.73,0.76]$ for $\lambda=1$ on a balanced design); in extrapolation it does not: tuned and fixed $\lambda=1$ are equal on a uniform design ($0.85$ and $0.85$) and tuning is slightly worse on balanced ($0.82$ against $0.78$) and PPS designs ($0.85$ against $0.82$).
The inner targets lie only just above the remaining sources, so the nested split imitates the real distance only partly.
A larger gap (the top half of the sources as inner targets), tried after seeing this result as a diagnosis, lowers the error of the logistic and kNN difference estimators by $4$--$6\%$ (paired ratio to the main inner split $0.94$ $[0.92,0.95]$ for balanced $\times$ difference IRT) and raises that of ridge by $9\%$ ($1.09$ $[1.08,1.11]$; Table~\ref{tab:robust}), so the shrinkage depends on how the inner split is built; we do not recommend the larger split.

\subsection{Does a win come from the design, the estimator, or both?}
\label{sec:res-ablation}

Figure~\ref{fig:ablation} (appendix) crosses six designs with the estimators.
Within each estimator column the designs that keep every item drawable and spread the sample over difficulty (stratified, PPS, balanced) are best and close to one another, and information-top selection is among the worst in every column, so a gain attributed to ``smart'' item selection depends on the estimator and the rule.
On a uniform design the difference estimator is better than the model-only IRT estimator by a factor of two in extrapolation ($0.85$ against $1.96$) and by about $10\%$ in interpolation ($0.70$ against $0.78$).
With the mean estimator, balanced selection improves on stratified sampling ($0.73$ against $0.81$ in interpolation, $0.81$ against $0.85$ in extrapolation), and with the difference estimator it improves on a uniform design by $3$--$5$ points of the ratio.
The adaptive IRT-CAT design is competitive in interpolation and lineage ($0.68$ and $0.67$ with the difference estimator) and not in extrapolation ($1.04$ $[0.91,1.10]$).

